    objectives: ['Distinguish population, community and habitat.', 'Identify biotic and abiotic influences.', 'Estimate abundance from representative quadrat samples.'],
    explanation: ['Ecology examines interactions among organisms and between organisms and their physical surroundings. A population consists of members of one species in a defined area and time; a community includes interacting populations. Biotic influences include predation and competition, while abiotic influences include temperature and water availability. These factors can interact, so a species’ distribution rarely follows one factor without qualifications.', 'Sampling allows a larger area to be estimated from manageable observations. Quadrats are useful for organisms that can be counted within a fixed area, such as many plants. Random or carefully justified stratified placement helps reduce selection bias. An estimate requires comparable sampling effort and an area that the sampled locations can reasonably represent; placing every quadrat beside an easy path can misrepresent the wider habitat.'],
    keyIdeas: ['Define the area and time for a population statement.', 'Biotic and abiotic factors can interact.', 'Representativeness matters as well as sample size.'],
    definitions: [{ term: 'Population', meaning: 'Members of one species in a defined area at a stated time.' }, { term: 'Community', meaning: 'The populations of different species interacting in an area.' }, { term: 'Habitat', meaning: 'The place and environmental conditions in which an organism lives.' }],
